---
/**
 * website/src/pages/getting-started.astro — getting started (website plan, Task W1.5;
 * website redesign, parts P4 and P5; the owner's draft of 2026-10-19, "Getting started,
 * draft 2 (OS icons)").
 *
 * Every sentence a reader acts on here is **rendered, not written**: the commands come
 * from `website/data/install.yaml` (W0.1), whose Go test pins each one to the file its
 * `source:` names — `README.md`'s `## Getting started` or `docs/release.md`'s
 * verification block — byte for byte, and the platform list to `README.md`'s
 * `## Requirements` and to `.goreleaser.yaml`'s `builds[0]`. The site is the fourth copy
 * of the install instructions to exist in this repository and the one most likely to be
 * read, so it is the copy that must not be a copy (finding 21, Global Constraint 6).
 *
 * **The page in three bands (the owner's feedback of 2026-10-19).** A reader who arrives
 * here wants one thing first: the command. So the page is ordered the way that reader
 * reads it, and each band answers one question.
 *
 *   1. **Above the fold: one card per system, each with its install command.** `#install`
 *      opens with a card for every platform `install.yaml` marks `supported` — macOS and
 *      Linux today — in the file's own order, and the card carries the path the file offers
 *      that system first, with that path's command and its copy control where the file gives
 *      the path a command (Linux's archive has none, so that card carries the note and the
 *      release page instead). The console's
 *      downloads sit in the macOS card, because the console ships for macOS (the owner's
 *      draft: the console's button belongs with the system it is built for). Nothing else is
 *      up there: no sentence about the page, no paragraph about where its words come from
 *      (D186). `website/test/fold.mjs` holds the ordering the claim rests on — the first
 *      command the section prints is in its first card — and Check 70b is the owner's
 *      measurement of the fold itself (D187).
 *   2. **"Set up your installation": what a real installation needs, in order.** The
 *      install command gets the CLI onto the machine; it does not create a project, a
 *      GitHub App or a secret. The steps below are `docs/gcp-setup.md`'s preconditions and
 *      its short path, in the order that document puts them, one line each, and each names
 *      the document that carries the detail rather than repeating it. No secret value is
 *      named, and no step is invented: every one of them is a precondition or a step of
 *      that runbook or of the plugin's `setup` skill, and the links are the only place the
 *      detail lives.
 *   3. **"Details": everything else, where it was.** The install paths the cards do not
 *      carry — cosign, `go install`, the plugin — keep the shape P4 gave them, with their
 *      own notes and citations, and the platform list keeps D94's statuses, Windows' own
 *      note among them. A reader who wants the verification scrolls to it; a reader who
 *      wants the command never had to.
 *
 * **The cards are derived from the file, and no card is named here.** A card is a platform
 * `install.yaml` marks `supported`, in the file's own order, and the path under it is the
 * first path whose own `platforms:` list carries that system's goos: the file's paths are in
 * the order a reader runs them, so the first one a system is offered is the one its card
 * carries. A system the file marks supported with no path under it, or a system with no
 * glyph in this file, stops the build naming the file rather than rendering a card with
 * nothing to run — and `website/test/os-cards.mjs` holds the built cards to the file from
 * outside, so a card that named a platform `install.yaml` does not carry fails there too.
 * One source of truth per fact (Global Constraint 6).
 *
 * **Windows is untested, and stays a note rather than a card** (D94, finding 20): a card is
 * an offer, and this repository builds no Windows binary. The platform list in Details
 * renders the status `install.yaml` carries, the Windows entry's own note included, and the
 * page carries no support promise the release build does not make.
 *
 * **The console is offered with the system it ships for.** Its downloads come from
 * `src/lib/console-downloads.mjs`, the one reader both pages use, and the card that renders
 * them is the one whose platform the console's own data file names on its assets. The join
 * is by the reader-facing name `install.yaml` carries, and a console whose assets name a
 * system this page draws no card for is a build error naming both files rather than a
 * download offered under the wrong system. While the console is unreleased the block
 * renders the data file's own sentence and no link but the one to `/console/`.
 *
 * Three things this page is written to say, and not to say:
 *
 *   - **The plugin path is a first-class path**, in Details rather than in a card: `fugaro
 *     init`, `/fugaro:setup` and `fugaro upgrade --local` are rendered with their own note,
 *     and the setup steps above them are where a reader meets the first two as steps
 *     (finding 22).
 *   - **The cosign verification is the archive's own facts, and it is a path in Details**
 *     (finding 19): the verification block is the release process' own text, and it sits
 *     with the paths no card carries. The archive is the Linux card's path — the macOS card
 *     carries Homebrew — and its own note names macOS too, so the tarball is offered to both
 *     systems; the commands that check it are the cosign path under "Other install paths",
 *     which is where a reader who took the archive finds them.
 *   - **The two cards are the two systems the file supports**, and the systems it does not
 *     are not offered: the page says nothing about a system it has no artifact for.
 *
 * **What the redesign took out (P4).** The page used to open with a paragraph about where
 * its commands come from — `install.yaml`, the Go test, the three files that test holds it
 * to — and every path carried "Commands as printed in <file>" and "Platforms: …" beside
 * its commands. A reader who wants to install Fugaro needs none of that: each note says
 * what the path is for, and the file named in the source line under each block is the whole
 * of the citation (D186: a page must not explain why its own text exists). The facts are
 * unchanged and still rendered from `install.yaml`; only the words around them went.
 *
 * Nothing here fetches: the page is built from the files in this repository, and the only
 * links it prints are the Releases page `install.yaml` names, the console's own published
 * assets, and this repository's own documents (Global Constraints 4 and 8).
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import ConsoleOption from '../components/ConsoleOption.astro'
import { SITE_ROOT, loadSite } from '../content.ts'
import { copyControlHTML } from '../lib/copy-control.mjs'
import { loadConsoleDownloads } from '../lib/console-downloads.mjs'

// The whole generated layer, read and validated through the base layout's own call: a
// missing install.yaml stops the build rather than rendering an empty install block
// (W1.3's pipeline).
const site = loadSite()
const install = site.install

// The console's own facts, from the one reader both pages use: the page renders the
// unreleased sentence from them while the console has no release, and the release's own
// published assets once it has — never a URL of its own (see the card below).
const consoleFacts = loadConsoleDownloads(
  SITE_ROOT,
  site.facts.features.consoleDownloads,
  site.facts.repository,
)

// The reader-facing name of a platform a path names. A path's own `platforms:` list names
// the **goos** a build produces — `darwin`, `linux` — while the platform list above carries
// the name a reader reads (`macOS`) for each of them, so the page translates through that
// list rather than printing an operating system's internal name at a reader. A path that
// named a goos the file does not describe renders the goos rather than inventing a name for
// it, so the mismatch is visible rather than papered over.
const platformNames = new Map(install.platforms.map((platform) => [platform.goos, platform.name]))

// What the repository builds for, and what it merely notes. `status` is the file's own
// word — `supported` or `untested` — and it is rendered as written (D94).
const platforms = install.platforms

const nameOf = (id: string): string => platformNames.get(id) ?? id

// The platforms a path is offered on, in the reader-facing names above: one line per path,
// short enough to sit beside the citation rather than under it.
const platformsOf = (ids: string[]): string => ids.map(nameOf).join(', ')

// The platforms a card is drawn for: the ones `install.yaml` marks `supported`, in the file's
// own order. `untested` is not a card — a card is an offer a reader can act on, and the file's
// Windows entry is a note about a system this repository builds nothing for (D94).
const cardPlatforms = install.platforms.filter((platform) => platform.status === 'supported')

// One card per supported system: the system, and the path the file offers it first. A system
// the file marks supported and carries no path for is a build error rather than a card with an
// empty body — the page offers what the file offers, and it has nothing to offer here.
const cards = cardPlatforms.map((platform) => {
  const path = install.paths.find((candidate) => candidate.platforms.includes(platform.goos))
  if (path === undefined) {
    throw new Error(
      `website/data/install.yaml marks ${platform.name} (${platform.goos}) supported and carries no install path for it: Getting Started draws a card per supported platform and each card carries the path the file offers that system first, so a supported system with no path is a card with nothing a reader can run`,
    )
  }
  return { platform, path }
})

// The paths the cards do not carry, in the file's own order: Details renders these, and a path
// a card already carries is not printed a second time.
const cardPaths = new Set(cards.map((card) => card.path.id))
const otherPaths = install.paths.filter((path) => !cardPaths.has(path.id))

// The two commands the setup steps name, read from the path that owns them rather than typed
// into a step: `fugaro init` and `/fugaro:setup` are the plugin path's own commands, and a step
// that printed its own spelling of either would be the fourth copy of the install instructions
// this page's header refuses. A file that stopped carrying them stops the build here, where the
// failure names the file, rather than rendering a step about a command that is not in it.
const pluginPath = install.paths.find((path) => path.id === 'plugin')
if (pluginPath === undefined || pluginPath.commands.length < 2) {
  throw new Error(
    "website/data/install.yaml carries no `plugin` path with its two commands (`fugaro init` and `/fugaro:setup`), which the page's setup steps render",
  )
}
const [initCommand, setupCommand] = pluginPath.commands

// The glyph a card is drawn with, keyed by the platform's own id in `install.yaml`: a laptop
// for macOS and a terminal for Linux — the two shapes the owner's draft draws. **No vendor's
// mark is reproduced here**: a system's icon is this page's own drawing, and a logo is not
// something this repository may copy. The shapes are inline markup and nothing else: no icon
// font, no image file, no third party, no `style` attribute, and nothing that names another
// document, so a page of this site draws its own picture and fetches nothing for it (Global
// Constraints 4 and 8).
const GLYPHS: Record<string, string> = {
  /* A laptop: the screen and the base it stands on. */
  macos: '<rect x="4" y="4.5" width="16" height="11" rx="1.5" /><path d="M1.5 19.5h21" />',
  /* A terminal: a window with a prompt and the line after it. */
  linux: '<rect x="3" y="4" width="18" height="16" rx="2" /><path d="M7.5 9.5 11 13l-3.5 3.5" /><path d="M13 16.5h3.5" />',
}

/** The shapes a glyph is drawn with: what has to be in an entry for it to draw anything. */
const GLYPH_SHAPES = /<(?:path|circle|ellipse|rect|line|polyline|polygon)\b/

{
  // Every card has a glyph, and it draws something. A supported platform the map does not carry,
  // or an entry with no shape in it, is an empty `<svg>` — a card whose icon is a blank box,
  // which is the defect a reader sees and no other gate can name.
  for (const { platform } of cards) {
    const glyph = GLYPHS[platform.id]
    if (glyph === undefined || !GLYPH_SHAPES.test(glyph)) {
      throw new Error(
        `website/src/pages/getting-started.astro: the card for ${platform.name} (${platform.id}, which website/data/install.yaml marks supported) is drawn with ${glyph === undefined ? 'no glyph' : JSON.stringify(glyph)}, and every card is drawn with one glyph that holds a shape: an entry this page does not carry, or one with nothing to draw in it, is a card with an empty icon`,
      )
    }
  }
  // One distinct drawing per card: two systems drawn with the same picture are one icon used
  // twice, and the picture then says nothing about the system under it.
  const drawings = new Set(cards.map((card) => GLYPHS[card.platform.id]))
  if (drawings.size !== cards.length) {
    throw new Error(
      `website/src/pages/getting-started.astro: the ${cards.length} cards are drawn with ${drawings.size} distinct glyph(s): each system's card carries a picture of its own, and two cards drawn alike are one icon used twice`,
    )
  }
}

// The system the console is offered with: the platform its own data file names on every asset
// it publishes — the console ships for macOS, so its downloads sit in macOS's card. The join is
// by the reader-facing name `install.yaml` carries, and both ways of failing to make it are
// build errors naming the files: a console whose assets name no system (or more than one) has
// no card to sit in, and one whose system this page draws no card for would be a download
// offered under a system it is not built for.
const consolePlatformNames = [...new Set(consoleFacts.downloads.map((download) => download.platform))]
if (consolePlatformNames.length !== 1) {
  throw new Error(
    `website/content/downloads.json names ${consolePlatformNames.length} platform(s) on its downloads (${consolePlatformNames.join(', ') || 'none'}), and Getting Started offers the console in the card of the one system it ships for: with no platform named there is no card to put the download in, and with two the page would have to decide which`,
  )
}
const consoleCard = cards.find((card) => card.platform.name === consolePlatformNames[0])
if (consoleCard === undefined) {
  throw new Error(
    `website/content/downloads.json publishes the console for ${consolePlatformNames[0]}, and website/data/install.yaml marks no supported platform by that name: Getting Started offers the console in that system's card, and a system this page draws no card for has no card to offer the download in`,
  )
}

// The steps a real installation needs, in the order `docs/gcp-setup.md` puts them. Each one
// is a precondition or a step of that runbook or of the plugin's `setup` skill, said in one
// line, with the link carrying the detail: the page states what to do and the document
// states how. `repo` is the repository `site.yaml` names, so a link into this repository is
// built from the site's own facts rather than typed here, and `href` is a heading of the
// document it points at (`website/test/linkcheck.mjs` refuses a fragment that is not one).
const doc = (path: string, fragment: string): string =>
  `${site.facts.repository}/blob/HEAD/${path}#${fragment}`
---

<Base title="Getting started">
  <h1>Getting started</h1>

  <p>Install Fugaro on macOS or Linux.</p>

  <section id="install">
    <h2>Install</h2>

    {/* The section opens with the cards themselves (the owner's draft of 2026-10-19): one per
        system `install.yaml` marks supported, in the file's own order, each carrying the path
        the file offers that system first — the macOS card's command is the first command on
        the page, which is what P5's fold claim is about and what `fold.mjs` holds. The one
        sentence the section carries moves with the order and closes the section, after the
        cards it is about: it is the way on to the steps below.

        The cards are one grid, in document order: macOS first, Linux second, which is the
        order the stacked layout renders at 390 px. */}
    <div class="install-cards">
      {cards.map(({ platform, path }) => (
        <article class="install-card" data-platform={platform.id} data-path={path.id}>
          {/* The system's glyph: inline, decorative, and drawn with the accent by the rule
              below (`stroke: currentColor` reads the `color` that rule sets). The drawing is
              the map's, not a branch here, so a card cannot be out of step with the map by one
              word and render an empty `<svg>` with nothing to see it. */}
          <svg
            class="os-icon"
            viewBox="0 0 24 24"
            fill="none"
            stroke="currentColor"
            stroke-width="1.75"
            stroke-linecap="round"
            stroke-linejoin="round"
            aria-hidden="true"
            focusable="false"
          >
            <Fragment set:html={GLYPHS[platform.id]} />
          </svg>

          <h3>{platform.name}</h3>

          {path.note !== '' && <p class="note">{path.note}</p>}

          {path.commands.map((command) => (
            <div class="command-block" data-copy>
              <Fragment set:html={copyControlHTML('Copy the install command')} />
              <pre><code>{command}</code></pre>
            </div>
          ))}

          {/* The line and the blocks above it are one fact: a path with no commands of its
              own — the release archive, whose facts are a page and a name template rather
              than a command line — renders neither, because "From README.md" over an empty
              list tells a reader to read something that is not there. install.yaml is
              allowed to carry a path with no commands, so a card has to render that shape as
              well as the other one. It follows the block it cites, so the command is the
              first thing under the path's name. */}
          {path.commands.length > 0 && (
            <p class="source">
              From <code>{path.source}</code> · {platformsOf(path.platforms)}
            </p>
          )}

          {path.releasePage !== '' && (
            <p class="release">
              <a href={path.releasePage} rel="noopener">{path.releasePage}</a>
            </p>
          )}

          {/* The console, offered in the card of the system it ships for and nowhere else:
              its own heading, its downloads once it has a release, and the link to the page
              that says what it is. While it is unreleased the component renders the data
              file's sentence and no download at all — the same rule the console page's
              Downloads section keeps, from the same facts. */}
          {platform.id === consoleCard.platform.id && (
            <ConsoleOption
              available={consoleFacts.available}
              note={consoleFacts.notReleased.short}
              downloads={consoleFacts.downloads}
              linux={consoleFacts.linux}
            />
          )}
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>

    <p>Then set up your installation.</p>
  </section>

  {/* What the command does not do. A reader who runs `brew install` has a binary and no
      installation: the project, the credentials, the App and the secrets are theirs to make,
      and `docs/gcp-setup.md` is the runbook that walks them through it. The steps below are
      that runbook's own preconditions and short path in its own order, one line each, and
      every one of them links to the document that carries it — the page states what to do
      and the document states how, so the two cannot drift. The two commands the last steps
      name are rendered from `install.yaml`'s plugin path rather than typed here, so those
      steps move with the file too. No secret is named here, and no value is anywhere on this
      site. */}
  <section id="setup">
    <h2>Set up your installation</h2>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <a href={doc('docs/gcp-setup.md', 'preconditions')}>A Google Cloud project</a>, billing linked, the Service
        Usage API on.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={doc('docs/gcp-setup.md', 'preconditions')}>gcloud credentials</a> with a quota project, and
        Terraform 1.9+.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={doc('docs/git-providers.md', 'github')}>A GitHub App</a> with four repository permissions.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={doc('docs/gcp-setup.md', 'turning-the-shared-budget-on-m9b')}>A Firebase project</a>, if you want
        the shared budget.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={doc('docs/gcp-setup.md', 'the-short-path-fugaro-init-then-fugarosetup')}>Run <code>{initCommand}</code></a> in your own terminal.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={doc('docs/gcp-setup.md', '4-a-repository')}>Store the secrets</a> the runs mount, at hidden
        prompts.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={doc('docs/gcp-setup.md', 'the-short-path-fugaro-init-then-fugarosetup')}>Run <code>{setupCommand}</code></a>,
        merge, run <code>{initCommand}</code> again.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  {/* Everything else, where it was: the paths the cards do not carry, each with its own note,
      its own citation and its own commands, and the platform list D94 settles — the untested
      system among them, as a note rather than a card. It is below the steps rather than beside
      them because the steps are what every reader needs and these are what a reader picks
      from. */}
  <section id="details">
    <h2>Details</h2>

    <h3>Other install paths</h3>

    <div class="path-list">
      {otherPaths.map((installPath) => (
        <article data-path={installPath.id}>
          <h4>{installPath.name}</h4>

          {installPath.note !== '' && <p>{installPath.note}</p>}

          {installPath.commands.map((command) => (
            <div class="command-block" data-copy>
              <Fragment set:html={copyControlHTML('Copy the install command')} />
              <pre><code>{command}</code></pre>
            </div>
          ))}

          {installPath.commands.length > 0 && (
            <p class="source">
              From <code>{installPath.source}</code> · {platformsOf(installPath.platforms)}
            </p>
          )}

          {installPath.releasePage !== '' && (
            <p class="release">
              <a href={installPath.releasePage} rel="noopener">{installPath.releasePage}</a>
            </p>
          )}
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>

    <h3>Platforms</h3>

    <ul>
      {platforms.map((platform) => (
        <li data-platform={platform.id} data-status={platform.status}>
          <strong>{platform.name}</strong> — {platform.status}
          {platform.note !== '' && <p class="note">{platform.note}</p>}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <style>
    /* The page's own box, and nothing else: the design is W1.2's, and every space, radius
       and colour below is a token from `src/styles/tokens.css` — never a value written
       here, and never a colour literal (D104, D105). Column widths are measured in `ch`
       rather than px because the measure is a typographic fact, not a device one. */
    section {
      margin-block: var(--space-xl);
      max-width: var(--measure);
    }

    /* The install section is the one section here that is wider than a measure: two cards
       side by side need the width, and the commands inside them are not prose. The one
       sentence the section carries is prose, and it keeps the measure. */
    #install,
    #details {
      max-width: none;
    }

    #install > p {
      max-width: var(--measure);
    }

    /* The steps are prose, one line each, and they keep the measure. */
    #setup ol {
      max-width: var(--measure);
    }

    #setup li + li {
      margin-top: var(--space-xs);
    }

    /* The cards, one column until the viewport has room for two: at 390 px this is the macOS
       card first and the Linux card under it, and the first command the section prints is in
       the first card, which is the page P5's fold claim is about. */
    .install-cards {
      display: grid;
      align-items: start;
      gap: var(--space-md);
    }

    @media (min-width: 48rem) {
      .install-cards {
        grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
      }
    }

    /* One system, one card: its glyph, its name, the path the file offers it and — where the
       console ships for it — the console's own block. Details' box, which holds the paths the
       cards do not carry, is the same box. */
    .install-card,
    .path-list {
      border: var(--border-width) solid var(--border);
      border-radius: var(--radius-md);
      padding: var(--space-md);
    }

    /* And inside the box the paths are sections of it rather than boxes in a box: the rule
       between two of them is the whole of the separation they need. */
    .path-list article {
      border: 0;
      border-top: var(--border-width) solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 0;
      padding: var(--space-md) 0 0;
      margin: var(--space-md) 0 0;
    }

    .path-list article:first-of-type {
      border-top: 0;
      padding-top: 0;
      margin-top: 0;
    }

    /* The glyph: one box per card, a step of the spacing scale, drawn a line above the name it
       belongs to. **The box is the glyph's box, and it is `display: block`** so a phone reader
       sees the icon on its own line rather than as an inline mark set into the name — and so
       the word-seam gate reads the boundary a reader sees (`website/test/spacing.mjs`). The
       accent is the stroke: `--accent` on `--ground` is a pair `a11y.mjs` computes at 3:1 in
       all four palettes, which is the ratio 1.4.11 asks of a graphic rather than prose. */
    .os-icon {
      display: block;
      width: var(--space-xl);
      height: var(--space-xl);
      margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
      color: var(--accent);
    }

    pre {
      background: var(--surface);
      overflow-x: auto;
      padding: var(--space-sm);
      border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    }

    .note,
    .source {
      color: var(--text-muted);
    }
  </style>
</Base>
